*Documentation System* | V-$CHIPVR ($CHIPCN)

The Chippy documentation system supports both static ''.chpdoc'' files and symbol extraction from source files.

*Search Order*

The documentation system searches paths in the following priority order:

1. ''doc/'' - Local development directory
2. ''~/.local/share/chippy/doc/'' - User-local documentation
3. ''/usr/share/doc/chippy'' - System documentation (managed by installer)

*Usage*

'''
chippy doc                        # List all available documents
chippy doc <document>             # Show specific document
chippy doc <file.chpdoc>          # Render .chpdoc file directly
chippy doc <file>                 # List symbols in source file
chippy doc <file> <symbol>        # Show symbol documentation
chippy doc <file> all             # Show all symbol documentation
'''

*Document Types*

The system handles three types of documentation:

1. *Static documents* - ''.chpdoc'' files searched by name in the documentation paths
2. *Direct files* - ''.chpdoc'' files are opened by path
3. *Source files* - ''.chp'' and ''.chh'' files with extractable ''#@'' documentation blocks

*Document Format*

Documentation files use the ''.chpdoc'' extension, which is custom. It is designed for simple rendering to a terminal and supports:

- *Bold text* using the * character
- ''Inline code'' highlighting using double apostrophes
- Version variables ''CHIPVR'' and ''CHIPCN'' (prefixed with ''$'') are expanded automatically
- Code blocks using triple apostrophes with line numbers:
'''
Like
this
'''

I recommend you look at ''/usr/share/doc/chippy'' for practical examples on how to use ''chpdoc'', it really is very simple.

*Symbol Documentation*

Source files can include inline documentation using the ''#@'' comment prefix:

'''
# Functions

#@ <FunctionName(param1, param2)>
#@ V-1.0.0 (2025-08-12)
#@
#@ Description text with ''inline code'' for parameters
#@ and *bold* for emphasis.

# Constants

#@ <CONSTANTNAME>
#@ (2025-08-12)
#@ Description of the constant.
'''

Generally there is no fixed format for how these comments are to be structured, the only thing required is the symbol definition ''<symbol>'' at the top of the comment. The examples above show how we do it in the corelib. See ''chippy doc chpdoc''.

*Third-party Integration*

Third-party packages can install documentation to ''~/.local/share/chippy/doc/'' without interfering with system documentation. This directory is not touched by the installer on reinstalls and upgrades. I don't recommend installing your documents to ''/usr/share/doc/chippy''.
